WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.519
Tomorrow, millions of homes, factories and trains will need electricity.

2
00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:08.877
Someone has to decide which power plants will produce it, and at what price.

3
00:00:09.353 --> 00:00:12.138
In this episode, we explore the day-ahead market:

4
00:00:12.168 --> 00:00:15.067
the auction that sets tomorrow's electricity price.

5
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.855
Electricity cannot be stored cheaply at large scale,

6
00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:23.111
and generation must match consumption at every moment.

7
00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:27.572
So the market lets everyone plan the next day in advance, before delivery.

8
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.388
Sellers include power plants, renewable producers and traders.

9
00:00:32.418 --> 00:00:37.252
Buyers include suppliers serving households and businesses, large consumers,

10
00:00:37.282 --> 00:00:38.020
and traders.

11
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.644
They all send orders to a power exchange.

12
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.719
Before gate closure, around midday on the day before delivery,

13
00:00:45.749 --> 00:00:50.143
every participant submits orders for each market time unit of the following day.

14
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:56.608
A sell offer states a quantity in megawatt-hours and the minimum price the seller will accept.

15
00:00:56.638 --> 00:01:00.732
A buy bid states a quantity and the maximum price the buyer will pay.

16
00:01:00.762 --> 00:01:04.378
After gate closure, one auction processes all orders at once.

17
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.456
Let's build the supply side with a simple example.

18
00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:10.005
Offer A:

19
00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:12.547
forty megawatt-hours at thirty euros.

20
00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:13.097
Offer B:

21
00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:14.128
forty at sixty.

22
00:01:14.158 --> 00:01:14.678
Offer C:

23
00:01:14.708 --> 00:01:16.670
twenty at one hundred twenty.

24
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.683
The auction sorts the offers from cheapest to most expensive.

25
00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.084
This is the merit order.

26
00:01:22.114 --> 00:01:25.822
Each block's width is its quantity, and its height is its price.

27
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:28.817
Now add demand:

28
00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:30.486
one hundred megawatt-hours.

29
00:01:30.516 --> 00:01:35.370
We treat it as price-insensitive, meaning buyers need this volume at any price.

30
00:01:35.853 --> 00:01:36.936
Walk up the stack.

31
00:01:36.966 --> 00:01:39.163
Offer A covers forty megawatt-hours.

32
00:01:39.193 --> 00:01:41.327
Offer B brings the total to eighty.

33
00:01:41.357 --> 00:01:45.842
Twenty are still missing, so offer C is needed, and it is fully accepted.

34
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.848
The last accepted offer is the marginal one,

35
00:01:48.878 --> 00:01:51.465
and its price sets the market-clearing price:

36
00:01:51.495 --> 00:01:53.965
one hundred twenty euros per megawatt-hour.

37
00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:01.027
In a uniform-price auction, every accepted offer is paid the same clearing price.

38
00:02:01.057 --> 00:02:06.159
Offer A earns forty times one hundred twenty, or four thousand eight hundred euros.

39
00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:07.580
Offer B earns the same.

40
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.053
Offer C earns two thousand four hundred.

41
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:12.217
The total is twelve thousand euros:

42
00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:15.432
one hundred megawatt-hours times one hundred twenty.

43
00:02:16.417 --> 00:02:19.688
Remember, this is a simplified single-period example.

44
00:02:19.718 --> 00:02:23.425
The real European auction uses an algorithm called EUPHEMIA,

45
00:02:23.455 --> 00:02:28.158
which handles many periods, order types and network limits at the same time.

46
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.961
Now watch the price react.

47
00:02:31.991 --> 00:02:36.716
If demand falls to seventy megawatt-hours, offer C is no longer needed,

48
00:02:36.746 --> 00:02:38.926
and the marginal offer becomes B.

49
00:02:38.956 --> 00:02:41.002
The price drops to sixty euros.

50
00:02:41.483 --> 00:02:44.880
Add forty megawatt-hours of wind at a very low price,

51
00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.078
and the stack shifts to the right.

52
00:02:47.108 --> 00:02:52.444
Offer C is rejected, offer B is only partly accepted, and the price is again sixty.

53
00:02:52.474 --> 00:02:55.354
These what-if cases use hypothetical numbers.

54
00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:00.328
That is why prices fall when renewable output is high or demand is low,

55
00:03:00.358 --> 00:03:02.613
and rise when cheap supply runs out.

56
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.329
Europe does not run these auctions in isolation.

57
00:03:07.359 --> 00:03:09.446
In the single day-ahead coupling,

58
00:03:09.476 --> 00:03:14.385
exchanges and grid operators run one joint auction across many bidding zones.

59
00:03:14.415 --> 00:03:17.785
Power flows from low-price zones to high-price zones,

60
00:03:17.815 --> 00:03:20.544
as long as interconnector capacity remains.

61
00:03:21.047 --> 00:03:23.787
If capacity is large enough, prices converge.

62
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:28.588
If the line is full, there is congestion, and the zones keep different prices.

63
00:03:28.618 --> 00:03:30.681
The numbers here are illustrative.

64
00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.576
In Serbia, the day-ahead market is operated by SEEPEX,

65
00:03:35.606 --> 00:03:37.608
the Belgrade-based power exchange.

66
00:03:37.638 --> 00:03:44.180
Serbia is a contracting party of the Energy Community and is working toward coupling with the European market.

67
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:49.019
Timelines have shifted before, so always check the latest official announcements.

68
00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:51.715
Three takeaways.

69
00:03:51.745 --> 00:03:51.973
One:

70
00:03:52.003 --> 00:03:56.630
the day-ahead auction lets the market plan tomorrow's supply and demand.

71
00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:56.889
Two:

72
00:03:56.919 --> 00:03:59.088
offers are stacked in merit order,

73
00:03:59.118 --> 00:04:02.709
and the last accepted offer sets the price for everyone.

74
00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:03.097
Three:

75
00:04:03.127 --> 00:04:07.236
coupling links zones, and congestion explains price differences.

76
00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.134
Next time, we move closer to delivery with intraday trading.

77
00:04:11.164 --> 00:04:13.091
Thanks for watching Power Academy.
